# Clear Glass gig file (clearglass.gig/1) — load it in Settings → Autofill → Gig, or in the gig panel.
# James, 2026-11-04: "can you make a gig for: "spec and architect your project regardless of size, and reduce overwhelm""
# The numbers in the description are from his own tree on 2026-11-04 (docs/*phasemap.spec: 201 maps, 782 phases; tests/modules: 534 test
# files). Change them when they change; never round them up.
format: clearglass.gig/1
platform: fiverr
title: I will spec and architect your project of any size and reduce overwhelm
category: Programming & Tech
subcategory: Software Development
# serviceType: the option exactly as Fiverr's "Service type" dropdown shows it for this subcategory (the fill picks it and checks it took)
tags:
  - architecture
  - technical spec
  - project planning
  - system design
  - roadmap
description: >-
  Is your project too big to hold in your head? Too many ideas and no clear next step? I turn it into a
  spec and an architecture you can build from, one piece at a time.


  I build my own large platform this way, spec-first: 201 build maps and 782 mapped phases, each naming
  its files, what it depends on, and the test that proves it is done. That is what keeps a big project
  calm and buildable, and it is what you get.


  What you get:

  - Your project in plain words: what it is for, who uses it, what "done" looks like

  - The parts of the system, what each one owns, and how they talk to each other

  - A dependency map, so you can see what must come first and what can wait

  - A build plan in small phases, the smallest useful version first

  - For each phase: what to build, what it needs, and how to test it

  - Risks named up front, before they cost you


  Small app or large platform, the method is the same: break it down until every next step is obvious.
  Any developer or AI coding tool can start from it straight away.
packages:
  basic:
    name: Clarity Brief
    description: 'Your project on one page: goal, users, core parts, and the first three steps.'
    deliveryDays: 2
    revisions: 1
    price: 60
    features: {}        # Fiverr's package rows for this subcategory, as the page labels them; the fill lists the rows it finds
  standard:
    name: Spec and Build Plan
    description: 'Full spec: parts, data, flows, dependency map, and a phased plan with tests.'
    deliveryDays: 5
    revisions: 2
    price: 180
    features: {}        # Fiverr's package rows for this subcategory, as the page labels them; the fill lists the rows it finds
  premium:
    name: Full Architecture
    description: Spec, architecture, phased plan, dependency graph and risk review, ready to build.
    deliveryDays: 10
    revisions: 3
    price: 420
    features: {}        # Fiverr's package rows for this subcategory, as the page labels them; the fill lists the rows it finds
faq:
  - question: My project is huge and messy. Is that a problem?
    answer: >-
      No, that is what this is for. I break it into parts and phases until each next step is small and
      clear, whatever the size.
  - question: I already have some code. Can you work with it?
    answer: >-
      Yes. I map what exists first, so the plan builds on your work instead of starting over.
  - question: Do I need technical knowledge?
    answer: >-
      No. You explain it in your words; I turn it into the technical plan and explain it back plainly.
  - question: Can an AI coding tool build from it?
    answer: Yes. It is written so that a developer or a tool like Claude Code can build it phase by phase.
requirements:
  - 'Describe your project: what it does, and for whom?'
  - What exists already? (an idea, notes, designs, code — share whatever you have)
  - What feels most overwhelming or stuck right now?
  - What must it always do, and what must it never do?
  - How will you know it is done? (what you will check or test)
